# List concatenation (`a + b`).
#
# Both operands come from temporaries wherever a reference could be dropped by
# mistake: the right-hand elements are owned *only* by the literal list, so a
# concatenate that forgets to retain them leaves the result pointing at freed
# boxes. That is the exact shape of the fray_add bug this case pins.

# 1. A literal on the right: its elements die with the temporary.
la = [1, 2]
lb = la + [3, 4]
print(lb)

# 2. Both operands temporaries — the result owns everything it points at.
lc = [10] + [20, 30]
print(lc)

# 3. Inside a nested block, where the old value is released as part of the
#    store rather than at the end of the block.
ld = [100]
if True:
    ld = ld + [200]
print(ld)

# 4. Accumulating in a loop: every round's right operand is a fresh temporary.
le = []
for k in range(4):
    le = le + [k]
print(le)

# 5. Concatenating two named lists, then dropping the originals: the result
#    must keep its own references to every element.
lf = [1, 2]
lg = [3, 4]
lh = lf + lg
lf = []
lg = []
print(lh)
print(len(lh))

# 6. A right operand nested one deeper, and an empty right operand.
li = [7]
lj = li + [[8, 9]]
print(lj)
print(lj[1][0])
lk = li + []
print(lk)
